AB  - Text-based occluded person re-identification (TO-ReID) is a key challenge in cross-modal person retrieval, aiming to retrieve occluded pedestrian images using natural language descriptions. Existing methods typically rely on token-level masking strategies, which often overlook high-level textual structures and struggle to recover discriminative features from severely occluded regions. To address these limitations, we propose a Text-Guided Occlusion-Aware Reconstruction framework (TOAR), which introduces a multi-level occlusion-aware mechanism to enhance cross-modal retrieval robustness. Specifically, we first design a Syntax-Guided Group Masking strategy that elevates masked language modeling from the token level to the syntax-guided semantic group level. This strategy encourages the text encoder to learn robust concept-level representations that are critical for fine-grained alignment. Second, we develop an Occlusion-Aware Feature Reconstruction (OAFR) module to recover occluded visual features by leveraging implicitly identified visible patches, which are regarded as reliable according to internal reconstruction confidence. By focusing reconstruction on these trustworthy regions and propagating their semantic context to occluded areas, OAFR restores discriminative visual content while maintaining semantic consistency with textual guidance, even under severe occlusion. Extensive experiments demonstrate that the proposed framework achieves state-of-the-art performance on challenging occluded TO-ReID benchmarks. These results validate the effectiveness of TOAR and highlight the importance of explicitly modeling occlusion through semantics-guided masking and feature reconstruction.
