AB  - Watermarking, which embeds imperceptible yet machine-detectable patterns into generated text, has emerged as a leading solution for detecting machine-generated content. While green-red watermarking methods embed detectable signals by biasing a “green” token subset, they remain vulnerable to paraphrasing attacks and context shifts: green tokens can be replaced, and edits to preceding tokens can disrupt the green-red partition. To address these limitations, we introduce DualMark, a novel ensemble-based dual-watermarking framework that employs an ensemble-inspired approach to embed two independent green-red watermarks using separate context windows and partitioning strategies. During generation, DualMark applies a tiered bias strategy, strongest to tokens in the intersection of both green lists while also biasing tokens unique to each list. Detection succeeds if either watermark yields a statistically significant signal. DualMark integrates seamlessly with existing watermarking schemes and incurs minimal overhead. Extensive experiments across various adversarial scenarios demonstrate that DualMark maintains text quality while substantially outperforming single-watermark baselines: it improves detection AUROC by over 11% under severe word-deletion attacks and doubles the true positive rate (TP@1%) against adaptive baselines under heavy paraphrasing, all while incurring only a 5% increase in generation latency.
