AB  - As global supply chain networks and financial markets grow increasingly complex, multimodal time series data naturally exhibits severe non-stationarity, cold-start bottlenecks, and impulsive shifts triggered by sudden events. Traditional forecasting models, whether they are pure numerical Transformer variants or emerging selective state space models, often exhibit structural inertia and a lack of logical adaptability when subjected to distribution shifts and data anomalies driven by external shocks. To address this, we propose CR-Mamba, a multimodal time series agent architecture that fuses intuitive prior judgments with rational dynamic evolution. Instead of deploying the Large Language Model (LLM) as an end-to-end forecaster, we repurpose it as a "logical scout" to extract lag-agnostic causal priors. We then pair these priors with the PCMCI algorithm to build a dynamic causal topology. To prevent the parameter explosion that typically plagues Kolmogorov-Arnold Networks in high-dimensional settings, we introduce a "Causal-Equivariant Grouping" strategy. By using topological isomorphism to guide parameter sharing and incorporating Rational Base Functions grounded in Padé approximation, we equip the Mamba-2 backbone with the ability to lock onto sudden pulses with an almost vertical response. Furthermore, to accurately quantify long-tail risks in supply chains, we mount a Conditional Flow Matching (CFM) decoder at the end of the architecture. Dynamically modulated by semantic cues, it uses ordinary differential equation (ODE) integration to reconstruct high-fidelity, non-parametric probability distributions. To defend against semantic noise like fake news, a built-in "Logical Self-Audit Module" uses a cross-modal confrontation mechanism to correct biases, securing robust decision-making even in extreme environments. Across three real-world scenarios—retail supply chains (Augmented M5), high-noise financial markets (StockNet), and energy grids (ECL-Weather)—CR-Mamba outpaces existing state-of-the-art baselines in both point prediction accuracy and probability calibration, all while maintaining an O(L) linear computational efficiency.
