AB  - Corrupted bitstream semantic understanding (CBSU) of images is a novel but challenging task in multimedia analysis. It involves understanding the underlying semantics of corrupted binary bitstream directly from the raw byte sequence fragments. The conventional paradigms of semantic understanding typically rely on the successful pixel decoding of bitstream. However, the corruption of bitstream may result in incomplete pixel decoding or even decoding failure, posing a significant challenge to semantic understanding. To solve the challenge, we propose CBSU-ALLM, a CBSU domain-specific large language model (LLM) that leverages the strong capabilities of perception, understanding, and reasoning of LLM. CBSU-ALLM treats binary bitstream fragments as a byte “language” modality. The underlying semantics of corrupted bitstream can be interpreted by adapting LLM on domain dataset. To this end, we construct the first large-scale CBSU domain dataset (CBSU-D). The CBSU-D contains 454k corrupted image bitstream, comprising multiple severity levels of corruption, multiple corruption types of bit-flip and byte-loss, from multiple data sources of MNIST, CIFAR-10, and STL-10. The benchmark on CBSU-D involves 10 state-of-the-arts (SOTA) LLMs for comparison. Our proposed CBSU-ALLM framework is also designed to support multimodal inputs to understand bitstream-image modalities and uses Modality Adaptive Encoding to adaptively extract pixels from corrupted bitstreams as an auxiliary modality. Experimental results show that CBSU-ALLM outperforms all SOTA comparison models in terms of all metrics, demonstrating the potentials of semantic understanding of corrupted bitstream by LLMs.
