AB  - Regulatory comment letters contain critical latent risk signals, yet extracting actionable insights from these lengthy, unstructured disclosures imposes significant information-processing costs on market participants. To bridge this gap, we introduce a novel large language model (LLM)-based framework to systematically decode, quantify, and categorize latent firm risks embedded in Chinese regulatory comment letters. Our approach captures the volume, economic materiality, and pervasiveness of firm-specific vulnerabilities, and is rigorously validated against human expert assessments and alternative LLMs. Empirically, we establish the economic relevance of these AI-decoded signals: Higher LLM-decoded risks are significantly associated with negative abnormal returns and suppressed trading volume, consistent with elevated processing constraints and investor “wait-and-see” behavior. Furthermore, contextualized within a framework of risk revelation and governance correction, our LLM measure serves as a powerful predictive indicator of subsequent corporate interventions. Specifically, severe risk signals robustly predict future financial restatements, elevated CEO/CFO turnover, and short-term profitability declines stemming from rectification frictions. Ultimately, this study illustrates the transformative potential of artificial intelligence in mitigating information asymmetry, enhancing capital market transparency, and empowering internal corporate governance.
