AB  - This paper evaluates whether single-prompt code generation by large language models produces behaviorally diverse scheduling heuristics for engineering expert-system deployment or collapses to a known classical rule. The application domain is identical parallel-machine makespan minimization, and the protocol uses 540 generations from six models and three prompt variants under a single-prompt, no-repair setting anchored on the longest-processing-time scheduling heuristic. A five-contract funnel screens executable deployability, and conforming schedulers are classified by a benchmark-level maximum-completion-time fingerprint on 2160 instances. Of 540 generations, 463 (85.7%) pass all contracts; among them, 461 of 463 (99.6%) are fingerprint-equivalent to longest-processing-time on the benchmark, without implying identical assignments or equivalence beyond the tested instances. The only benchmark-distinct conforming class, longest-processing-time followed by first-improvement pairwise-swap local search, reduces mean exact regret on adversarial instances from 5.02% to 0.53%. Reasoning-mode inference increases visible token cost by at least 22.31 times without adding behavioral diversity. A post-hoc anchor-variation experiment is more consistent with a training-prior explanation than with pure in-context imitation, but is not causal evidence. The artificial-intelligence contribution is a contract-validated behavioral-fingerprinting screen for large language model code synthesis. The engineering contribution is a regime-dependent validation protocol for expert-system scheduling, showing when one-shot synthesis is adequate and when explicit algorithmic structure or local search is needed.
