AB  - Low-rank adaptation (LoRA) is a popular method for efficiently fine-tuning pre-trained language models (PLMs) with lightweight, trainable plugins. This paradigm is vulnerable to backdoor attacks, in which attackers embed triggers that force the LoRA-tuned model to produce attacker-chosen outputs. However, existing defenses focus on backdoors in the main PLM backbone, leaving LoRA plugins unaddressed. When these backbone-oriented defenses are applied to LoRA-tuned models, they erase task-specific knowledge while degrading model performance, resulting in the defense-convergence dilemma. To address this issue, we propose selective layer-wise cleansing (SLC), a framework for removing backdoors from LoRA plugins while maintaining task performance. SLC is based on our backdoor activation divergence principle, which states that benign and malicious inputs result in statistically distinct layer-level activation patterns. Our framework first trains a benign auxiliary model on publicly available, task-agnostic data to serve as a clean behavioral baseline. It then identifies anomalous layers in the target model by comparing their activation patterns. Finally, SLC uses a layer-wise distillation process to transfer verified, task-specific knowledge into the clean auxiliary model, guided by the divergence ranking. Experiments show that SLC solves the defense-convergence dilemma. Compared to representative baselines, such as PURE, RECIPE, and BDEKD, SLC robustly defends against various backdoor attacks while maintaining high accuracy on natural language understanding (NLU) benchmarks. The code for our proposed SLC is released at https://github.com/AIR6200/SLC.
