AB  - Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) have demonstrated remarkable successes across various downstream tasks but are increasingly vulnerable to visual adversarial attacks. Existing defenses, such as denoising-based or training-based approaches for traditional vision tasks, are unsuitable for safeguarding LVLMs due to their high inference costs or the unaffordable training requirements of large-scale models and datasets. To address these challenges, we propose PromptGuard, a novel defense mechanism leveraging adversarial prompt tuning to enhance the robustness of LVLMs without requiring extensive model training or additional inference overhead. By exploiting cross-modality components (e.g., Q-former) embedded in LVLMs, we tune learnable prompts to align image features from clean and adversarial inputs using a well-designed loss function. This approach mitigates adversarial features on the inputs while minimizing the side effects of tuned prompts. We validate the effectiveness of PromptGuard against state-of-the-art adversarial attacks on several classical tasks. The experimental results demonstrate that PromptGuard effectively defends LVLMs in a cost-efficient manner, improving accuracy by up to 32.8% under attack, with only a 7.9% increase in inference cost. This work introduces a novel perspective on adversarial robustness enhancement for LVLMs, showcasing the potential of prompt tuning as a lightweight and scalable defense mechanism.
