AB  - Open-vocabulary semantic segmentation (OVSS) aims to recognize arbitrary categories by transferring the generalization capability of vision-language models such as CLIP. However, existing training-free approaches mainly refine the final layer attention, overlooking the hierarchical structure of the CLIP encoder. This leads to the loss of multi-scale local cues from middle layers and may leave final-stage dense representations over-homogenized, weakening local patch distinctiveness. To address these limitations, we propose MiddleCLIP, a framework designed to enhance both local preservation and structural fidelity. It introduces two plug-and-play modules that can be seamlessly integrated into existing OVSS methods, delivering more accurate segmentation results. Local Preserved Middle Feature Fusion (LPMF2) fuses shallow and middle layer features to recover local spatial details. Structure-Aware Complementary Feature Fusion (SACF2) constructs complementary final-stage paths to alleviate over-homogenized attention responses while retaining feed-forward structural refinement. Together, they form a balanced representation that captures fine-grained structures without compromising global semantics. We conduct extensive experiments across eight benchmarks to validate the effectiveness. The proposed method consistently outperforms recent baselines across various backbones, showing consistent effectiveness in both with- and without-background settings. The codes are available at https://github.com/XiaoJinNK/MiddleCLIP.
