DO  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2026.133033
UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0957417426019445
KW  - Adversarial examples
KW  - Black-box
KW  - Natural language processing
KW  - Multi-hop
AB  - Most existing character-level and word-level textual adversarial attacks focus on simple perturbations, which often do not utilize the associative abilities of human beings in real-world. In this paper, we propose a new framework for generating adversarial examples through multi-hop semantic perturbations, which generates a multi-hop association graph for Chinese and English words based on human associative paradigms for building a search space of potential adversarial examples. To search for the optimal adversarial examples in the large discrete search space, we design a discrete particle swarm optimization algorithm with chaotic mapping, which is incorporated to prevent the particle swarm optimization from converging prematurely. We also explore two methods, including dynamic adversarial training and graph-based recovery, to shield systems from multi-hop semantic perturbations proposed in our paper. Extensive experiments on six datasets show that advanced models and commercial applications are vulnerable to our multi-hop semantic perturbations in black-box settings, and even large language models like GPT-4 suffer up to a 57% drop in accuracy across different conditions, whereas other baselines perform far worse overall.
