UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2772375526003825
KW  - Image-text-based diagnosis
KW  - Foundation model
KW  - Patch-based image analysis
KW  - Plant disease diagnosis
KW  - Disease severity estimation
KW  - Decision support system
AB  - Dogwood (Cornus florida) is an economically valuable ornamental tree in North America, but leaf spot diseases reduce aesthetic quality and market value and are difficult to diagnose because symptoms overlap across pathogens. This study presents SpotChecker, an open-source, web-based decision-support application that prioritizes interpretable disease diagnosis and management guidance using a fine-tuned language model, supported by transformer-based image segmentation for quantitative disease severity estimation. A GPT-2 Medium language model was fine-tuned using LoRA on 3336 disease-specific question-answer (QA) pairs covering six major dogwood leaf spot diseases. The model generates the likely disease, causal pathogen, and concise management recommendations from user prompts, achieving 95% accuracy on a limited evaluation set of 60 manually constructed prompts. This design addresses the practical needs of growers, who often seek actionable guidance. To provide objective disease severity metrics that support and contextualize language-based recommendations, a transformer-based Mask2Former semantic segmentation framework was developed to delineate canopy and leaf spot regions from smartphone images. Standard segmentation architectures (U-Net, DeepLabV3+, and Feature Pyramid Network (FPN)) were first evaluated under a consistent patch-based training pipeline and showed limited performance in detecting small, sparse lesion regions. To address this limitation, a transformer-based Mask2Former framework was adopted and further extended with task-specific strategies. A dataset of 1100 annotated dogwood canopy images collected across the 2023–2024 seasons was used for training and evaluation. The Mask2Former framework was further extended using an inside-canopy two-class formulation, leaf-centered patch sampling, and an auxiliary binary cross-entropy (BCE)-Focal Tversky loss to address extreme lesion sparsity. Across controlled ablations (A2-A5), the final configuration improved validation leaf spot Intersection over Union (IoU) from 0.58 to 0.70, achieving test-time IoU values of 0.70 under patch-based evaluation and 0.60 under full-image sliding-window inference. Using segmentation outputs, SpotChecker computes continuous disease severity (%) as the proportion of predicted leaf spot pixels within the canopy. Across the test set, ground-truth severity ranged up to 3.46%, and SpotChecker produced severity estimates that closely matched ground truth across severity groups (mean predicted: 0.46%, 1.11%, and 2.06% for mild, moderate, and extreme vs 0.35%, 0.89%, and 2.11% ground truth, respectively), supporting objective tracking of disease progression and fungicide effectiveness. SpotChecker integrates these components in a Streamlit interface hosted on Hugging Face Spaces, allowing growers and practitioners to upload a smartphone image and receive both objective severity estimates and actionable, pathogen-aware guidance to support timely management decisions.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
T1  - Using LLMs to enhance code quality: A systematic literature review
AU  - Alomari, Nawaf
AU  - Redah, Moussa
AU  - Ashraf, Ahmad
AU  - Alshayeb, Mohammad
JO  - Information and Software Technology
VL  - 190
SP  - 107960
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026/02/01/
SN  - 0950-5849
DO  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.infsof.2025.107960
UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S095058492500299X
KW  - Code quality
KW  - LLM
KW  - systematic literature review
AB  - Context:
Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly used in software engineering to enhance code quality through tasks such as refactoring and code smell detection and many other tasks. Code smells are poor design decisions that can be resolved by changing the internal structure of the code without affecting its output, a process known as refactoring.
Objective:
This study systematically reviews the use of LLMs in code quality enhancement, focusing on techniques such as refactoring, smell detection, and other code improvement methods.
Method:
Using SLR techniques, we reviewed 49 studies up to September 2024, analyzing both qualitative and quantitative data to assess trends and effectiveness.
Results:
The field is active, with refactoring as the most common task, followed by smell detection. Refactored code by LLMs is not reliable. Prompting is used more frequently than fine-tuning, with few-shot learning as the leading prompting method. Java and Python are the most represented languages, while F1, Precision, Recall, and Accuracy are common evaluation metrics, along with BLEU and EM for generation tasks. Open-source and general language models are preferred, with validation datasets as the primary validation approach.
Conclusions:
LLMs show promise for code quality improvement, but challenges in optimization and reliability remain. Future research should prioritize fine-tuning for refactoring, linking LLMs to specific quality attributes, developing benchmark datasets, constructing datasets for diverse programming languages, and exploring a wider range of promoting techniques.
ER  - 

