DA  - 2026/09/01/
SN  - 0925-2312
DO  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neucom.2026.133829
UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0925231226012269
KW  - Visual object tracking
KW  - Data augmentation
KW  - Vision-language models
KW  - Multimodal feature enhancement
AB  - Previous object tracking methods based on visual image patches have achieved strong performance but suffer from limited representational capacity, especially in the presence of background clutter or novel targets. To overcome this, we propose a dual-branch enhancement method, which includes: (1) an Occlusion&Mix module that iteratively updates the background of the target and partially occludes the target through region-level image mixing to encourage the model to learn discriminative target features in a cluttered background, and (2) a visual-language enhancement module that combines semantic features obtained from a vision-language model (VLM) with visual features, aiming to capture both explicit visual similarity and implicit semantic relevance to facilitate a deeper understanding of the target. The proposed modules are used only during the training stage, with inference identical to the baseline tracker. We conducted experiments on four large-scale tracking datasets and two challenging benchmark datasets focused on different attributes (such as semantic understanding, motion blur, occlusion, and so on). Compared to the baseline, it especially improves the Precision score by 2% on the LaSOText dataset, SR0.75 score by 4.1% on the GOT10k dataset, and AUC score by 2.3% on the NFS dataset, achieving performance competitive with state-of-the-art trackers.
